1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
Thank you.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,980
Thank you.

3
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,640
In our lives and in this space and to create space for him to grab a hold of our attention

4
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:07,500
and our hearts.

5
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:09,040
I pray that the worship has done that.

6
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:11,180
I pray that the Scriptures will do that.

7
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:12,740
I pray that our fellowship will do that.

8
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:15,700
If we haven't had the privilege of meeting yet, my name is Ryan.

9
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:19,760
I had the privilege of being one of the pastors here, and we have the privilege of being in

10
00:01:19,760 --> 00:01:27,540
a series on the book of Ruth, a very small narrative testimony in the Old Testament that

11
00:01:27,540 --> 00:01:31,120
is slowly but surely becoming one of my favorite books in the Old Testament.

12
00:01:31,120 --> 00:01:34,040
We started with an overview of how the book works.

13
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:40,220
It is a story about two, three individuals who are in the midst of their own suffering, and

14
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:46,180
God is kind of behind the scenes at work bringing about a plan of redemption for their whole

15
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:46,800
lives.

16
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:50,820
But it's also not just a story about these individuals, but a story about God's redemption

17
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:52,020
in the whole world.

18
00:01:53,460 --> 00:01:59,340
It's about how God is working through the messiness of our broken lives to bring about his redemption.

19
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:03,040
I quoted one pastor, and we're going to keep coming back to this quote, who said,

20
00:02:03,040 --> 00:02:08,540
the book of Ruth is like the story of the whole Bible told in four chapters through three characters.

21
00:02:08,540 --> 00:02:13,940
It's the story of God working out his seemingly painstakingly slow redemption in the midst of

22
00:02:13,940 --> 00:02:14,460
our suffering.

23
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:22,360
And it's a redemption that is set against the context of eternity.

24
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:25,160
I think we get so consumed.

25
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:29,280
Good thing there's no dogs in the room.

26
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:33,140
That would be a problem in my household.

27
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:37,660
See how it goes.

28
00:02:37,660 --> 00:02:40,420
We got the best of the best back there, so they'll figure it out.

29
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:41,220
They already figured it out.

30
00:02:41,220 --> 00:02:45,340
It's the redemption that's set against the context of eternity.

31
00:02:45,340 --> 00:02:50,840
We get so consumed trying to see the redemption in our own earthly lives, and that often comes.

32
00:02:50,840 --> 00:02:52,380
We often see God answer prayers.

33
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:58,240
We often see God's people rally together to partner with God in bringing his redemption

34
00:02:58,240 --> 00:02:59,360
to our earthly worlds.

35
00:02:59,360 --> 00:03:01,680
But redemption isn't just earthly.

36
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:02,260
It's eternal.

37
00:03:02,260 --> 00:03:03,700
Our lives are but a vapor.

38
00:03:03,700 --> 00:03:06,360
We're here one day, and we're gone the next.

39
00:03:06,360 --> 00:03:12,660
And God's plan of redemption is eternal and centered on the resurrection of Jesus, giving us a confident

40
00:03:12,660 --> 00:03:17,580
expectation that even if we don't experience redemption in the ways in which we're hoping and praying

41
00:03:17,580 --> 00:03:27,300
for God's plan for on this earth, that we will experience it for all of eternity in resurrected bodies, free from the curse of the fall, fully redeemed when heaven comes to the earth.

42
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:30,660
So I do have to give a warning this morning, just in case.

43
00:03:30,660 --> 00:03:35,460
As we get to Ruth chapter 3, it is going to be a little bit PG-13.

44
00:03:37,660 --> 00:03:43,440
There's some feet being uncovered, and some people are running out of the room right now.

45
00:03:43,440 --> 00:03:44,360
I'm not ready for that.

46
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:49,040
A little PG-13, and at times it might be emotionally heavy.

47
00:03:49,040 --> 00:04:01,660
There's some topics that are covered in the background of Ruth chapter 3 that I want to discuss that is charged with a lot of emotion and pain that some of us in the room have felt and are experiencing.

48
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:06,360
And so I just want to kind of set the stage so that that doesn't sneak up on you when we get to it.

49
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:07,440
Sound good?

50
00:04:07,440 --> 00:04:09,080
All right, let's pray.

51
00:04:09,080 --> 00:04:20,960
Abba, you are a good father.

52
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:27,020
You are working about your good redemption plan in our lives, whether we can see it or not.

53
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:34,760
Not only are you working redemption in our lives, but you are inviting us to participate in the process of redemption.

54
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:48,300
You are inviting us to surrender our will and our ways, our definitions of good, so that we can partner alongside of you with your definition of good.

55
00:04:48,300 --> 00:04:52,520
To see your kingdom come and your will be done on earth as it is in heaven.

56
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:54,640
God, Ruth is such a beautiful picture of that.

57
00:04:57,080 --> 00:04:58,220
And yet it falls.

58
00:04:58,220 --> 00:05:04,120
And yet it shadows something far greater that comes along in the gospel.

59
00:05:04,120 --> 00:05:08,460
Jesus, fully God and fully man.

60
00:05:08,460 --> 00:05:13,080
Bringing about our ultimate redemption through his death, burial, and resurrection.

61
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:16,020
May you fix our eyes on that reality this morning.

62
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:20,700
May it grip our hearts and our minds.

63
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:23,580
May it not just be an intellectual belief that we ascend to.

64
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:27,680
But a reality that changes everything.

65
00:05:27,680 --> 00:05:30,300
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

66
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:31,300
Amen.

67
00:05:31,300 --> 00:05:35,900
Well, my beautiful baby boy just turned two this week.

68
00:05:35,900 --> 00:05:39,580
And I forgot I was going to put a picture up.

69
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:41,220
Man, that's a bummer.

70
00:05:41,220 --> 00:05:44,680
I would have made the sermon ten times better than anything that I would have to say.

71
00:05:44,680 --> 00:05:46,080
He's an amazing kid.

72
00:05:46,080 --> 00:05:47,200
It's such a fun age.

73
00:05:47,260 --> 00:05:51,800
He's learning so much, loving to explore all that this world has to offer.

74
00:05:51,800 --> 00:05:55,980
And he currently has two phrases that impact our daily lives.

75
00:05:55,980 --> 00:05:57,520
Very much so.

76
00:05:57,520 --> 00:06:02,140
The two phrases are by self and I help.

77
00:06:02,140 --> 00:06:07,800
By self means that he wants to do everything he possibly can do by himself.

78
00:06:08,180 --> 00:06:11,540
Whether or not he's actually capable of doing it by himself or not.

79
00:06:11,540 --> 00:06:12,440
He wants to try.

80
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:18,040
It means that everything he wants to do by self takes five times longer than it would if he let us help him.

81
00:06:18,040 --> 00:06:23,460
And not only that, but we also have to stay very close to him when he's doing things by self.

82
00:06:23,460 --> 00:06:28,500
Because it's very possible that he could harm himself or others while he's doing things by self.

83
00:06:29,220 --> 00:06:30,540
The second is I help.

84
00:06:30,540 --> 00:06:31,920
He wants to help us do everything.

85
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:34,400
From mopping the floor to folding the clothes.

86
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:37,480
And I have to be honest with you, pastoral confession here.

87
00:06:37,480 --> 00:06:42,360
When I see him running towards me when I have a basket of clothes in my hand saying, I help.

88
00:06:42,360 --> 00:06:47,100
I try and find something to distract him so that he doesn't help.

89
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:50,380
Because his help is actually somewhat unfolding the clothes.

90
00:06:50,380 --> 00:06:52,100
But we love it.

91
00:06:52,100 --> 00:06:55,180
And the other day he wanted to make scrambled eggs with his mother.

92
00:06:55,180 --> 00:06:57,760
I help.

93
00:06:58,920 --> 00:07:04,580
Not only did he want to help make scrambled eggs with his mother, but he wanted to help make scrambled eggs that nobody asked for or wanted.

94
00:07:04,580 --> 00:07:07,440
He just wanted to make scrambled eggs at seven o'clock in the evening.

95
00:07:07,440 --> 00:07:14,720
And so my wife, who is a lovely, patient mother, said, why not?

96
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:16,280
Let's make some scrambled eggs together.

97
00:07:16,280 --> 00:07:24,520
And I got to sit back and observe this sweet time of communion and connection and partnership that ended with a successful batch of scrambled eggs.

98
00:07:25,460 --> 00:07:29,120
Along with a few unsuccessful broken yolks on the ground.

99
00:07:29,120 --> 00:07:38,860
And I share this story with you because as we continue to look at God's redemption plan unfolding in the lives of Ruth, Naomi, and Boaz.

100
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:45,140
We're going to see it is a redemption plan that God is inviting his people into.

101
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:52,280
And I think these two phrases help us to see how our participation often works in God's redemption plan.

102
00:07:52,640 --> 00:08:12,540
See, although by self is a very healthy phrase for a toddler gaining independence to use, when it comes to the kingdom of God, it is a phrase that represents the fall of man and the curse of this broken world.

103
00:08:12,540 --> 00:08:25,920
Our Abba in heaven, our good father created everything in this world, inviting us to partner with him, to cultivate it with him, according to his good and loving principles as a loving father.

104
00:08:25,920 --> 00:08:27,320
And in return, we rebelled.

105
00:08:27,320 --> 00:08:30,440
We turned our back on God's presence and his principles.

106
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:37,860
And we began to cultivate his creation by ourselves, according to our own selfish and corrupt desires.

107
00:08:38,320 --> 00:08:40,800
And these desires lead to chaos and destruction.

108
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:48,180
We lie, steal, cheat, lust, abuse, manipulate to get what we want out of this world.

109
00:08:48,180 --> 00:08:52,100
And we don't typically care about how it affects other people.

110
00:08:52,100 --> 00:08:56,600
This is actually how the Bible defines God's wrath.

111
00:08:56,600 --> 00:09:03,340
In Romans chapter 1, the Bible says that God's wrath is allowing us to experience the consequences of us doing life on our own.

112
00:09:04,260 --> 00:09:14,500
It's God saying, if this is what you want, if you're not going to receive my invitation to do life with me, if you are going to go out on your own and live life by yourself, I will let you do that.

113
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:21,380
But just know that when you do, it will lead to your destruction and the destruction of others.

114
00:09:21,380 --> 00:09:23,760
It might lead to momentary pleasures.

115
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:26,300
It might lead to earthly gain.

116
00:09:26,400 --> 00:09:32,260
But at the end of the day, it will lead to pain and consequences for you and others.

117
00:09:32,260 --> 00:09:38,000
On the other hand, this phrase, I help, represents our participation in God's redemptive plan.

118
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:45,520
Even after our rebellion, even after looking at the God who created us and loved us in the face and saying, I don't want to partner with you.

119
00:09:45,520 --> 00:09:46,740
I want to do it on my own.

120
00:09:46,740 --> 00:09:56,160
He continually and regularly invites his people, all people, to lay down their previous life of doing it by themselves and to come into partnership with him.

121
00:09:56,160 --> 00:10:03,820
He invites us regularly to partner with him through obedience to his kingdom and reliance on his grace and forgiveness when we fail.

122
00:10:05,420 --> 00:10:16,840
And I think for me, this image of Marissa making eggs with Rory will be in my memory for a long time because it's a beautiful picture of what our relationship with God is like when we choose to participate in his redemption plan.

123
00:10:16,840 --> 00:10:30,000
It's an invitation to do good work alongside our father who loves us and has our best interest and the best interest of those around us in mind.

124
00:10:30,700 --> 00:10:43,040
And the reality is when we pull up a chair and say, I help with God, there are going to be times when we, out of our own selfish desires or out of our own excitement or out of our own zeal, we'll smash a couple eggs and they'll still end up on the ground.

125
00:10:43,040 --> 00:10:44,260
It's not a perfect process.

126
00:10:44,260 --> 00:10:45,320
We are broken people.

127
00:10:45,320 --> 00:11:00,680
But at the end of the day, if we stick with it, if we stick by his side, looking to him for guidance and wisdom and direction, I believe ultimately we will see redemption in our lives and the lives of those around us.

128
00:11:01,260 --> 00:11:05,680
And this is the overarching theme I want to explore in chapter three of the book of Ruth.

129
00:11:05,680 --> 00:11:09,380
It's that God will get redemption done.

130
00:11:09,380 --> 00:11:14,260
We've seen it, we've seen it in this, to our time in the book of Ruth.

131
00:11:14,260 --> 00:11:15,620
We see it all throughout the Scriptures.

132
00:11:15,620 --> 00:11:22,980
God will get redemption done, whether you and I participate or not, whether we are, nope, by self, I don't need you.

133
00:11:22,980 --> 00:11:23,720
I don't want you.

134
00:11:23,760 --> 00:11:24,940
I'm going to do life on my own.

135
00:11:24,940 --> 00:11:33,740
Or whether we seek to participate with him and help, even though, even in our helping, we often make things worse because we're fallen and we're broken.

136
00:11:33,740 --> 00:11:37,900
God will get redemption done, whether we participate or not.

137
00:11:37,960 --> 00:11:47,520
But the key reality I want us to focus on this morning is that there is a whole lot less collateral damage in our lives and in the lives of those around us when we choose to participate with him.

138
00:11:49,360 --> 00:11:54,420
So, to recap, before we start reading, this chapter is shorter, so hopefully we'll end on time.

139
00:11:54,420 --> 00:12:01,940
Ruth and Naomi are suffering widows, doing their best to survive with no real hope for a future outside of purely surviving.

140
00:12:01,940 --> 00:12:02,760
Imagine that.

141
00:12:03,040 --> 00:12:03,780
Some of you can.

142
00:12:03,780 --> 00:12:08,920
Imagine the reality of waking up each day just with the sole purpose of surviving.

143
00:12:08,920 --> 00:12:10,420
There's no hope for a future.

144
00:12:10,420 --> 00:12:14,900
There's no thought of anything outside of how are we going to stay alive today.

145
00:12:14,900 --> 00:12:18,000
This is the situation that Ruth and Naomi are in.

146
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:24,560
Chapter 2, Ruth steps out in faith, trusting in the character of God that she has recently come to know and love.

147
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:29,800
And she begins to glean in the fields, hoping to gather enough leftovers just for her and Naomi to survive.

148
00:12:30,860 --> 00:12:40,560
And through the providence of God, she ends up in the field of a man named Boaz, who embodies the chesed love of God, the covenant faithful love of God for the widow, the orphan, and the foreigner.

149
00:12:40,560 --> 00:12:51,260
Ruth steps out in faith again, and she makes a bold request of Boaz that will exceed what God's law requires of him, and he is blown away by her understanding of the heart of God.

150
00:12:51,260 --> 00:12:53,320
He goes above and beyond to her request.

151
00:12:53,320 --> 00:12:56,960
She comes home, if you remember last week, with two weeks worth of wages.

152
00:12:56,960 --> 00:12:59,580
God's redemption through human participation.

153
00:12:59,840 --> 00:13:10,760
God, at work, through the sacrificial love of Boaz and through the bold faith of Ruth, God is beginning to redeem Ruth and Naomi's stories in ways that they could never even imagine.

154
00:13:12,460 --> 00:13:25,000
And although this physical provision was a massive step forward for Ruth and Naomi, without a head of household and a patriarchal culture, they are still lacking a lot of security, a lot of hope for any real future.

155
00:13:25,160 --> 00:13:28,040
Yes, they have physical provision, but that's about it.

156
00:13:28,040 --> 00:13:30,280
They still don't have a hope for their future.

157
00:13:30,280 --> 00:13:35,340
And that's where chapter two begins to wind down.

158
00:13:35,340 --> 00:13:40,600
And the author, as the author is going to do in chapter three, the author leaves us with a cliffhanger.

159
00:13:40,600 --> 00:13:42,200
Everybody loves cliffhangers, right?

160
00:13:42,200 --> 00:13:48,140
Anybody get sucked into binge watching a show way too late because each show ends with a new cliffhanger?

161
00:13:48,240 --> 00:13:52,500
They didn't actually answer any of the questions that they wanted you to—anybody else?

162
00:13:52,500 --> 00:13:53,620
That's me.

163
00:13:53,620 --> 00:13:56,800
For some reason, I'm addicted to shows that never actually resolve.

164
00:13:56,800 --> 00:14:00,000
They just cliffhang, cliffhang, cliffhang.

165
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,180
And that's what Ruth did—the author of Ruth did in chapter two.

166
00:14:03,700 --> 00:14:11,640
She leaves this cliffhanger where Naomi begins to get a glimmer of hope from Ruth's faithful actions.

167
00:14:11,640 --> 00:14:17,820
And at the end of chapter two, she mentions that Boaz is not just a generous man, but he's also a close relative,

168
00:14:17,820 --> 00:14:24,320
which makes him one of their guardian redeemers, a role that's outlined in the Old Testament law and the customs of the day,

169
00:14:24,320 --> 00:14:29,320
which encourages close-blood relatives to help families out when they fall on hard times.

170
00:14:30,160 --> 00:14:33,220
The chapter comes to the close with this big cliffhanger.

171
00:14:33,220 --> 00:14:34,220
What will happen?

172
00:14:34,220 --> 00:14:38,560
Is this just a job that Ruth and Naomi have, or is this something far greater?

173
00:14:38,560 --> 00:14:46,860
Is this something that will redeem not just their physical lives, but the family lineage that we've lost in chapter one?

174
00:14:46,860 --> 00:14:50,700
So let's look at chapter three this morning in four scenes.

175
00:14:51,340 --> 00:14:57,340
And the first scene, I'm calling Naomi's plan, verses one through four.

176
00:14:57,340 --> 00:15:03,340
So we end with this cliffhanger, and it begins in verse three, starting in verse one.

177
00:15:03,340 --> 00:15:05,820
One day, Ruth's mother-in-law, Naomi, said to her,

178
00:15:05,820 --> 00:15:10,200
My daughter, I must find a home for you where you will be well provided for.

179
00:15:10,200 --> 00:15:14,900
Now, Boaz, with whose women you have worked, is a relative of ours.

180
00:15:15,400 --> 00:15:18,900
Tonight, remember the PG-13 warning, all right?

181
00:15:18,900 --> 00:15:22,100
Tonight, he will be winnowing barley on the threshing floor.

182
00:15:22,100 --> 00:15:25,120
Wash, put on perfume, and get dressed in your best clothes.

183
00:15:25,120 --> 00:15:27,120
Then go down to the threshing floor.

184
00:15:27,120 --> 00:15:31,200
But don't let him know you're there until he has finished eating and drinking.

185
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:35,900
Okay, this is in the Bible, people.

186
00:15:35,900 --> 00:15:39,900
When he lies down, note the place where he is lying.

187
00:15:39,900 --> 00:15:43,400
Then go and uncover his feet and lie down.

188
00:15:43,400 --> 00:15:46,180
In the Bible, people.

189
00:15:46,180 --> 00:15:46,640
Okay.

190
00:15:46,640 --> 00:15:49,960
He will tell you what to do.

191
00:15:49,960 --> 00:16:01,260
Now, one thing I want to point out here that I think is really significant is we see in chapter one that Naomi is totally done with God, right?

192
00:16:01,320 --> 00:16:05,300
She's coming back to Israel because she doesn't feel like she has any other hope at all.

193
00:16:05,300 --> 00:16:07,120
But she says, don't call me Naomi.

194
00:16:07,120 --> 00:16:10,780
Call me Mara, for the Lord has dealt bitterly with me.

195
00:16:10,780 --> 00:16:12,840
She is just absolutely done.

196
00:16:12,840 --> 00:16:14,720
Where is God's redemption in my life?

197
00:16:14,720 --> 00:16:16,780
How has he allowed this to happen to me?

198
00:16:16,780 --> 00:16:18,240
She is done.

199
00:16:18,240 --> 00:16:23,280
And in chapter two, I pointed out that Naomi's physically capable to take a 50-mile walk back home.

200
00:16:23,280 --> 00:16:29,980
But she doesn't even have the hope to go glean, which is the thing that would provide her life.

201
00:16:31,320 --> 00:16:39,540
But what I want to see here at chapter three is chapter three mirrors chapter two, except this time the one taking the action is Naomi.

202
00:16:39,540 --> 00:16:52,180
It's almost like the faith of Ruth, the hope of Ruth demonstrated on behalf of both Ruth and Naomi is starting to wake Naomi up to have a bit of hope for her future more than she did before.

203
00:16:53,060 --> 00:16:56,420
Now, Naomi begins to play the role of Ruth's mother here.

204
00:16:56,420 --> 00:16:59,020
Remember, their mother-in-law, her son's dead.

205
00:16:59,020 --> 00:17:01,740
At this point, legally, they're just friends.

206
00:17:02,400 --> 00:17:14,420
But she decides that she's going to take on the burden of a parent finding a partner, finding a marriage for Ruth, seeking to arrange a marriage that will set Ruth and her family up for the future.

207
00:17:14,420 --> 00:17:18,960
She knows that Ruth and Boaz have been spending a lot of time together in the fields.

208
00:17:19,500 --> 00:17:22,660
And not only that, Boaz is Naomi's close relative.

209
00:17:22,660 --> 00:17:33,340
And this is an opportunity for them to forge a marriage that would provide for Ruth in ways that she has no expectation of moving forward.

210
00:17:33,340 --> 00:17:36,640
This wouldn't be outside of the realm of possibility.

211
00:17:36,640 --> 00:17:41,740
Given the circumstances, it seems like it might be a match made in heaven.

212
00:17:41,740 --> 00:17:43,400
That was a pun.

213
00:17:43,400 --> 00:17:47,220
You guys love my jokes.

214
00:17:47,220 --> 00:17:47,780
I know you do.

215
00:17:49,120 --> 00:17:59,680
However, the plan Naomi proposes, I don't know about you, but to me it seems a bit risque at best and totally inappropriate at worst.

216
00:17:59,680 --> 00:18:10,480
It looks on the surface like Naomi is asking Ruth to potentially offer sexual favors to Boaz for the sake of continued and increased redemption for their family.

217
00:18:10,480 --> 00:18:11,280
It's interesting.

218
00:18:11,280 --> 00:18:14,100
Wait until he finishes eating and drinking?

219
00:18:15,740 --> 00:18:19,620
All right, there's some stuff going on there.

220
00:18:19,620 --> 00:18:30,300
And although I believe, I truly believe the narrative is intentionally charging the scene with the potential for sexual exploitation and sexual inappropriateness,

221
00:18:30,300 --> 00:18:34,600
leaving the audience very curious for how things will turn out.

222
00:18:34,600 --> 00:18:36,080
This is a great screenwrite.

223
00:18:37,080 --> 00:18:42,800
What is going to happen at the threshing floor tonight in the dark after some wine has been drank?

224
00:18:42,800 --> 00:18:47,640
What is going to happen to this main character that we've grown to love?

225
00:18:47,640 --> 00:18:53,060
I think what Naomi and certainly what Ruth have in mind is far beyond sexual favors.

226
00:18:53,060 --> 00:19:01,920
It's a hope of a legitimate marriage that will lead to their ultimate redemption and long-term security for both Ruth, Naomi, and their family line.

227
00:19:01,980 --> 00:19:09,980
This phrase, wash, perfume, and dress in your best clothes, it looks like she might be trying to just make herself as appealing as possible to Boaz.

228
00:19:09,980 --> 00:19:11,960
But I think there's more than meets the eye.

229
00:19:11,960 --> 00:19:20,540
I think she is transitioning from a widow in mourning to making herself, yes, appealing, but also signaling to Boaz that she is now available for marriage.

230
00:19:21,500 --> 00:19:29,760
When widows were dressed in their clothing for widow in mourning, it was very much a sign to all eligible bachelors that I am not on the market.

231
00:19:29,760 --> 00:19:31,660
I'm not available for marriage.

232
00:19:31,660 --> 00:19:36,520
But when they come out of this season of mourning and when they wash, perfume, change their clothes,

233
00:19:36,520 --> 00:19:42,120
they're making this visible action to those around them that might be eligible for marriage that I am now available.

234
00:19:42,120 --> 00:19:48,140
This is a bold and risky plan.

235
00:19:51,500 --> 00:19:59,640
It's a vulnerable woman putting herself at the feet of a wealthy man in a position of power over her.

236
00:19:59,640 --> 00:20:03,780
It is bold and it is risky, but it is not unfounded.

237
00:20:03,780 --> 00:20:11,460
It's a plan that is rooted in the faith of Yahweh to redeem and the confidence that they have seen in the character of God demonstrated through Boaz.

238
00:20:11,460 --> 00:20:17,100
They're not putting themselves at the feet of somebody that they don't know and haven't seen demonstrate the character of God.

239
00:20:17,100 --> 00:20:25,400
Both Ruth and Naomi have seen Boaz's character lived out and they're taking a risk, but they're taking a risk based on circumstances that they've seen in his life.

240
00:20:25,400 --> 00:20:28,460
They're almost saying we trust him with our vulnerability.

241
00:20:28,460 --> 00:20:33,240
We trust him to do the right thing, even when no one's around.

242
00:20:34,300 --> 00:20:46,080
So the main application point I want to make from this scene is that the biblical narrative seems to indicate that God loves to use people who are willing to take bold and sometimes risky steps of faith.

243
00:20:46,080 --> 00:20:52,500
From Abraham leaving everything he knew at the promise of God, making him the father of many nations.

244
00:20:52,500 --> 00:21:02,760
From David, a little shepherd boy who picked up a rock and a slingshot in front of an entire army of strong and powerful men who were deathly afraid to go up against Goliath.

245
00:21:02,760 --> 00:21:14,220
David says, I'm going to take a bold and risky action from Abraham to David to Elijah who dumps water, who literally dumps water on an altar and calls down fire from heaven.

246
00:21:14,220 --> 00:21:15,080
That's pretty bold.

247
00:21:15,080 --> 00:21:20,480
That's pretty risky from the disciples dropping their nets or leaving the tax collector booth.

248
00:21:20,480 --> 00:21:24,200
Everything they knew to participate in God's redemptive plan.

249
00:21:24,200 --> 00:21:27,700
It often takes bold, seemingly reckless acts of faith.

250
00:21:31,700 --> 00:21:42,780
And the reality is back to the yolks, this is, we're going to keep coming back to the egg yolks on the floor because as much as it's a beautiful image that will be in my head forever, I also will remember the yolks on the floor for a while as well.

251
00:21:42,780 --> 00:21:50,700
Sometimes we take bold steps of faith in a direction that we think is God's only to realize it was misguided.

252
00:21:50,700 --> 00:21:54,280
Peter chopping off a soldier's ear in the garden.

253
00:21:54,280 --> 00:21:54,700
Anybody?

254
00:21:55,700 --> 00:21:55,960
Right?

255
00:21:55,960 --> 00:22:09,140
Like, sometimes this attitude that leads us into bold faith, to obedience to God's kingdom, sometimes the same attitude of bold faith leads us to do some things that we actually weren't invited to do by God in the first place.

256
00:22:09,940 --> 00:22:22,280
However, God seems to be more interested in active participants who hop in the boat with him and start rowing, even if sometimes they start rowing in the wrong direction, than people who are unwilling to take the risk of getting in the boat at all.

257
00:22:22,280 --> 00:22:26,380
Or, worst case, decide to build their own boat instead.

258
00:22:30,220 --> 00:22:37,060
This is the words of Christ when he says, whoever wants to save their life will lose it, but anyone who loses their life for my sake will find it.

259
00:22:37,060 --> 00:22:44,280
And I got to be honest with you all this morning, this is a hard word for me because I don't know about you, but I'm a safety first kind of guy.

260
00:22:44,280 --> 00:22:49,160
I am a preserve and protect at all costs kind of guy.

261
00:22:49,160 --> 00:23:00,560
And yet, when we look at the grand narrative of Scripture, it often seems like God is inviting his people to take bold, risky acts of faith to participate with him in his redemption plan.

262
00:23:06,560 --> 00:23:19,400
Following Jesus and participating in his redemption plan works best when we aren't crippled by fear, always seeking to preserve our safety, waiting for him to show up in miraculous ways instead of pulling up the chair with prayerful action and saying, I help.

263
00:23:19,400 --> 00:23:21,280
I'm with you.

264
00:23:21,280 --> 00:23:22,620
Let's do this together.

265
00:23:22,620 --> 00:23:25,900
Naomi steps out in faith with a bold plan.

266
00:23:25,900 --> 00:23:28,380
Let's see how Ruth is going to respond.

267
00:23:28,380 --> 00:23:29,940
Verse five.

268
00:23:29,940 --> 00:23:34,140
She's asked her to do some pretty bold things.

269
00:23:34,480 --> 00:23:36,780
And her response is, I will do whatever you say.

270
00:23:36,780 --> 00:23:42,800
So she went down to the threshing floor and did everything her mother-in-law told her to do.

271
00:23:42,800 --> 00:23:43,980
She washed.

272
00:23:43,980 --> 00:23:45,660
She perfumed.

273
00:23:45,660 --> 00:23:46,660
She got ready.

274
00:23:46,660 --> 00:23:52,780
She went down to the threshing floor in the dark, hiding from anybody being able to see her.

275
00:23:52,780 --> 00:23:58,240
And when Boaz had finished eating and drinking and was in good spirits.

276
00:23:58,240 --> 00:24:02,060
Don't have time to comment.

277
00:24:02,660 --> 00:24:05,580
He went over to lie down at the far end of the grain pile.

278
00:24:05,580 --> 00:24:09,480
Ruth approached quietly, uncovered his feet and lay down.

279
00:24:09,480 --> 00:24:12,240
In the middle of the night, something startled the man.

280
00:24:12,240 --> 00:24:13,360
Yeah, I bet.

281
00:24:13,360 --> 00:24:19,440
He turned and there was a woman lying at his feet.

282
00:24:19,440 --> 00:24:20,720
Who are you?

283
00:24:20,720 --> 00:24:21,260
He asked.

284
00:24:21,260 --> 00:24:23,380
I am your servant, Ruth.

285
00:24:23,380 --> 00:24:29,840
She said, spread the corner of your garment over me since you are a guardian redeemer of our family.

286
00:24:30,340 --> 00:24:39,840
Ruth responds with her own bold and risky faith, doing everything Naomi suggests and even more.

287
00:24:41,040 --> 00:24:49,800
And last week, I rode pretty hard against this narrative that Boaz was immediately attracted to Ruth and that that was the only reason that he was being generous.

288
00:24:49,800 --> 00:24:57,980
And although I think Boaz's initial act of generosity towards Ruth was rooted in sacrificial love of God and neighbor, they've likely spent a lot of time together.

289
00:24:59,120 --> 00:25:05,300
And Ruth likely thinks that there's at least a chance that he's interested in order for her to go make this action.

290
00:25:05,300 --> 00:25:10,880
It's risky faith, but it isn't blind or void of logic.

291
00:25:10,880 --> 00:25:23,480
Oftentimes partnering with God and his redemptive plan for the kingdom is us paying attention with prayerful hearts, asking the Holy Spirit to lead, guide and direct us, and then making the next best step moving forward.

292
00:25:24,360 --> 00:25:26,580
So this is what Ruth does.

293
00:25:26,580 --> 00:25:35,000
Now, there's a lot of confusing theories around exactly what it meant in this cultural context for Ruth to uncover Boaz's feet.

294
00:25:35,000 --> 00:25:40,080
And I know many of you have been asking me for weeks what exactly I think is happening there.

295
00:25:40,660 --> 00:25:44,380
I'm going to pull the pastor card and avoid answering it specifically.

296
00:25:44,380 --> 00:25:57,980
I don't want to get lost in the weeds on that this morning, but I think the overarching point is that Ruth is positioning herself at the feet of a man who's able to provide for her as a vulnerable person.

297
00:25:57,980 --> 00:26:03,000
She's about to position herself to make a very vulnerable proposal to him.

298
00:26:05,420 --> 00:26:09,400
Also, it practically functions as the thing that would wake him up in the middle of the night.

299
00:26:09,400 --> 00:26:15,300
Like, at the threshing floor, they've just harvested all of their grain.

300
00:26:15,300 --> 00:26:18,240
This is everything that he has from the season.

301
00:26:18,240 --> 00:26:22,080
He is there in the middle of the night to protect it.

302
00:26:22,080 --> 00:26:27,860
So if somebody wakes him up by, like, grabbing his shoulders, he's probably got a sword beside him.

303
00:26:27,860 --> 00:26:34,380
And Ruth might not get to make the proposal that she's intending to make if she wakes him up in any crazy way.

304
00:26:34,380 --> 00:26:42,320
And so not only is she positioning herself, and it is, the scholars are clear that it is charged with some sexual tension.

305
00:26:42,320 --> 00:26:44,900
I think the author is doing that on purpose.

306
00:26:44,900 --> 00:26:46,880
We'll get to that in a little bit.

307
00:26:46,880 --> 00:26:53,420
But for now, it practically functions as the thing that would wake him up from a deep sleep after a night of celebrating the harvest.

308
00:26:53,900 --> 00:26:59,260
He responds with the question, who are you, which makes a ton of sense given the moment, right?

309
00:26:59,260 --> 00:27:01,820
But I want you to underline that question.

310
00:27:01,820 --> 00:27:03,780
We're going to come back to that question at the very end.

311
00:27:03,780 --> 00:27:05,460
For now, we'll move on.

312
00:27:05,460 --> 00:27:07,100
It's a good practical question.

313
00:27:07,100 --> 00:27:13,260
If any of you have ever been out in the dark where there's no light pollution, I mean, it'd be lucky if he even saw her silhouette.

314
00:27:13,260 --> 00:27:17,620
Now, this is where Ruth goes a bit off script from Naomi's plan.

315
00:27:17,620 --> 00:27:21,500
Naomi says, you lay there, you wake him up, you wait for him to tell you what to do.

316
00:27:21,500 --> 00:27:23,240
That's not Ruth's style.

317
00:27:23,900 --> 00:27:24,880
And I like it.

318
00:27:24,880 --> 00:27:25,620
She's spunky.

319
00:27:25,620 --> 00:27:26,780
She is bold.

320
00:27:26,780 --> 00:27:30,860
She moves forward with confidence in the character of God and the character of Boaz.

321
00:27:30,860 --> 00:27:32,880
She does not wait to see what Boaz will do.

322
00:27:32,880 --> 00:27:37,920
She takes center stage in this moment and goes above and beyond what Naomi had in mind.

323
00:27:37,920 --> 00:27:43,700
See, I think Naomi, at the root of it all, Naomi had marriage in mind for Ruth.

324
00:27:43,700 --> 00:27:48,800
And Ruth has something much better in mind for Naomi and for the family line.

325
00:27:48,900 --> 00:28:02,320
She's not just seeking a marriage, but she's also seeking him to function as the guardian redeemer, to take in both her and Naomi, to redeem the family land and to restore the lineage of her dead husband.

326
00:28:02,420 --> 00:28:11,160
This is a massively bold request that would require a ton of sacrifice and economic stress on Boaz in this situation.

327
00:28:11,160 --> 00:28:13,460
She takes the center stage.

328
00:28:13,460 --> 00:28:14,560
She goes above and beyond.

329
00:28:18,880 --> 00:28:24,300
Now, just like last week, Ruth is asking Boaz to go above and beyond what the law required.

330
00:28:24,300 --> 00:28:27,880
Boaz was not required by the law of God to do any of this.

331
00:28:27,880 --> 00:28:44,360
There are things in the Old Testament that talk about the closest relative, but there's principles and there's laws and Boaz isn't required to do any of this.

332
00:28:44,360 --> 00:28:47,600
And so she's looking at the heart of God.

333
00:28:47,600 --> 00:28:56,920
She's looking at the spirit of the law and she's looking at the character of Boaz and she's inviting him to go once again above and beyond what is required of him.

334
00:28:56,920 --> 00:29:00,920
And to see the heart of God and to provide for them in incredible ways.

335
00:29:00,920 --> 00:29:08,060
She spent time with Boaz and she's seen the heart of God reflected in him and she's asking him to participate in God's redemption plan.

336
00:29:08,060 --> 00:29:12,020
I love the terminology she uses.

337
00:29:12,020 --> 00:29:14,700
Again, not only is she spunky and bold, but she is witty.

338
00:29:14,700 --> 00:29:23,720
Look at this phrase, spread the corner of your garment over me for you are a guardian redeemer of our family.

339
00:29:23,720 --> 00:29:27,260
May this term spread the corner of your garment over me.

340
00:29:27,260 --> 00:29:35,980
It harkens back to a prayer that Boaz prayed over her in chapter two, verse 12, where he says, may the Lord repay you for what you have done.

341
00:29:35,980 --> 00:29:41,720
May you be richly rewarded by the Lord, the God of Israel under whose wings you have come to take refuge.

342
00:29:41,720 --> 00:29:44,720
Corner of your garment, same terminology.

343
00:29:44,720 --> 00:29:49,720
Ruth is asking Boaz to be the answer to the prayer that he prayed for her.

344
00:29:49,720 --> 00:29:54,660
He's saying, you prayed for God to protect me, to take care of me.

345
00:29:54,660 --> 00:29:57,160
You asked God on behalf of me to do that.

346
00:29:57,160 --> 00:30:03,260
And now I am asking you to partner with God and be the instrument by which that prayer is answered.

347
00:30:03,760 --> 00:30:06,600
This is incredibly bold and creative.

348
00:30:06,600 --> 00:30:13,580
God loves to partner with his people to accomplish his redemption.

349
00:30:13,580 --> 00:30:15,620
It's the theme throughout the book of Ruth.

350
00:30:15,620 --> 00:30:19,040
Now, second application point I want to make, and I have to make it quick.

351
00:30:21,660 --> 00:30:28,320
And it's an emotionally charged topic that has split churches all throughout our country.

352
00:30:28,320 --> 00:30:30,700
And so we're not going to get into the nuances of it.

353
00:30:30,700 --> 00:30:39,520
But throughout the book of Ruth, we see Ruth and Boaz partnering with one another and with God for the redemption of their family.

354
00:30:39,520 --> 00:30:47,540
But what I want to point out to you to this point in chapters two and chapters three is that Ruth is primarily in the driver's seat.

355
00:30:48,140 --> 00:30:51,920
She plays the lead role while Boaz plays the supporting role.

356
00:30:51,920 --> 00:31:08,020
Again, I don't have time to get into all of the specifics, but I just want to say that I think many streams of the church in America today might be missing out on some things that God wants to do in them and through them because they have limited the voice and leadership gifts of women in their communities.

357
00:31:08,020 --> 00:31:16,000
Before the fall, God put Adam and Eve in the garden and invited them both to rule over creation together.

358
00:31:16,000 --> 00:31:20,260
Read Genesis 1 and 2 and look at what God invites Adam and Eve to do together.

359
00:31:20,260 --> 00:31:24,100
He gives them dominion over creation to rule it together.

360
00:31:24,100 --> 00:31:25,580
This is God's ideal.

361
00:31:25,580 --> 00:31:31,560
When heaven and earth were together, men and women were meant to work in harmony with one another's strengths.

362
00:31:31,560 --> 00:31:37,000
Ruth's strength and leadership does not weaken Boaz's ability to lead.

363
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:38,460
It strengthens it.

364
00:31:38,460 --> 00:31:43,200
Boaz becomes a better leader as him and Ruth work together in their strengths.

365
00:31:43,640 --> 00:31:55,960
As we partner together with God and his redemption plan in the world, my hope and my prayer is that we can learn to create healthy spaces where men and women can learn to lead together in mutuality and submission to one another.

366
00:31:55,960 --> 00:31:58,080
I believe this is God's ideal.

367
00:32:00,080 --> 00:32:03,580
All right, next emotionally charged, difficult topic.

368
00:32:03,580 --> 00:32:05,860
I really made it easy for myself this week.

369
00:32:05,860 --> 00:32:08,180
Scene 3, Boaz's response.

370
00:32:08,180 --> 00:32:11,580
And this is the crux of the script.

371
00:32:11,580 --> 00:32:14,020
This is what the author is leading towards.

372
00:32:14,020 --> 00:32:15,720
This is where the tension is all going.

373
00:32:16,520 --> 00:32:22,520
They've presented this sexually charged situation with a man in power over a vulnerable woman.

374
00:32:22,520 --> 00:32:27,300
And the author, the audience is wondering what is going to happen when no one is around.

375
00:32:27,300 --> 00:32:28,360
Right?

376
00:32:28,360 --> 00:32:32,440
We've seen what Boaz will do for Ruth in the public square.

377
00:32:32,440 --> 00:32:37,020
But what will Boaz do for Ruth when his reputation isn't on the line?

378
00:32:37,020 --> 00:32:41,220
When he can pretty much get away with anything he wants to get away with in this situation?

379
00:32:41,420 --> 00:32:43,720
How will Boaz respond?

380
00:32:43,720 --> 00:32:47,700
Verse 10, the Lord bless you, my daughter.

381
00:32:47,700 --> 00:32:51,680
He replied, this kindness is greater than that which you showed earlier.

382
00:32:51,680 --> 00:32:54,780
You have not run after the younger men, whether rich or poor.

383
00:32:54,780 --> 00:32:56,960
And now, my daughter, do not be afraid.

384
00:32:56,960 --> 00:32:58,880
I will do for you all that you ask.

385
00:32:58,880 --> 00:33:02,760
All the people of my town know that you are a woman of noble character.

386
00:33:02,760 --> 00:33:06,220
Although it is true that I am a guardian redeemer of your family,

387
00:33:06,220 --> 00:33:09,400
there is another who is more closely related than I.

388
00:33:09,480 --> 00:33:11,940
I see the author setting up another cliffhanger for us.

389
00:33:11,940 --> 00:33:13,080
This is great.

390
00:33:13,080 --> 00:33:15,840
Stay here for the night and in the morning,

391
00:33:15,840 --> 00:33:19,120
if he wants to do his duty as your guardian redeemer, good.

392
00:33:19,120 --> 00:33:20,060
Let him redeem you.

393
00:33:20,060 --> 00:33:23,660
But if he is not willing, as surely as the Lord lives, I will do it.

394
00:33:23,660 --> 00:33:25,880
Lie here until the morning.

395
00:33:25,880 --> 00:33:28,320
So she lay at his feet until morning,

396
00:33:28,320 --> 00:33:30,940
but got up before anyone could be recognized.

397
00:33:30,940 --> 00:33:34,520
And he said, no one must know that a woman came to the threshing floor.

398
00:33:34,520 --> 00:33:35,780
That would be awkward.

399
00:33:35,780 --> 00:33:37,320
Rumors would start.

400
00:33:37,320 --> 00:33:38,920
Gossip would go, would grow.

401
00:33:39,440 --> 00:33:42,600
He also said, bring me the shawl you are wearing and hold it out.

402
00:33:42,600 --> 00:33:46,100
I pause there for dramatic effect.

403
00:33:46,100 --> 00:33:46,960
Nothing weird is going to happen.

404
00:33:46,960 --> 00:33:47,300
All right.

405
00:33:47,300 --> 00:33:52,480
Bring me the shawl you are wearing and hold it out.

406
00:33:52,480 --> 00:33:57,080
When she did so, she poured into it six measures of barley and placed the bundle on her.

407
00:33:57,080 --> 00:33:59,700
Then he went back into town.

408
00:34:01,900 --> 00:34:06,320
So again, one of the overarching themes of chapter three is the same as chapter two.

409
00:34:06,320 --> 00:34:09,540
God is in the business of partnering with people to bring about his redemption.

410
00:34:09,540 --> 00:34:16,560
Last week, it was in the public square where lack of generosity could have negatively impacted Boaz's communal image.

411
00:34:16,560 --> 00:34:19,460
But this week, it's in the secret place when no one will find out.

412
00:34:19,460 --> 00:34:24,540
No one has to know how Boaz handles the sexually tense situation.

413
00:34:27,540 --> 00:34:36,740
This is the primary question the authors are wondering and Boaz continues to demonstrate his godly character and puts our fears to bed immediately.

414
00:34:36,740 --> 00:34:47,980
The first words out of his mouth address Ruth as daughter, probably immediately lowering her heart rate and making her realize that she is in the safe space that she thought she was in.

415
00:34:47,980 --> 00:34:50,800
He praises her for her continued godly character.

416
00:34:50,800 --> 00:34:54,580
He obviously believes she has plenty of other options for marriage.

417
00:34:54,580 --> 00:34:56,780
He says you didn't chase after.

418
00:34:56,860 --> 00:35:00,300
And this is probably the best textual argument for the fact that Boaz is attracted to her.

419
00:35:00,300 --> 00:35:03,820
He's like, you could have had anybody, but you chose me.

420
00:35:03,820 --> 00:35:18,340
And you chose me not only because of whatever feelings you have towards me, but you also sacrificially chose me because you know I am the person who can not only take care of you, but also Naomi and also restore the family lineage of your dead husband.

421
00:35:18,340 --> 00:35:23,940
This is an act of sacrificial love on Ruth's part and Boaz knows it.

422
00:35:25,900 --> 00:35:36,220
And then Boaz uses the same adjective to describe her that the narrator uses to describe Boaz when he says that she is a woman of noble character.

423
00:35:36,220 --> 00:35:45,500
She's a woman of excellence in the community of Israel.

424
00:35:45,500 --> 00:35:53,480
Boaz isn't just looking at her as a widow in need of a husband, but he is looking at her as a peer.

425
00:35:53,480 --> 00:35:58,080
He uses the exact same terminology that others have used of him.

426
00:35:58,080 --> 00:36:03,400
He's saying this is a match made and nailed it.

427
00:36:03,400 --> 00:36:04,180
We nailed it.

428
00:36:04,180 --> 00:36:07,000
All right.

429
00:36:07,000 --> 00:36:14,660
Boaz ensures Ruth's safety and keeps the plan moving forward smoothly by keeping her there through the night because he doesn't want to send her home in the dark when it would be dangerous.

430
00:36:14,660 --> 00:36:18,900
But he wants to send her home a little bit early so that nobody else sees what's happening.

431
00:36:18,900 --> 00:36:21,720
You all know what the what the saying says.

432
00:36:21,720 --> 00:36:29,880
What happens on the threshing floor gets recorded in Scripture for the entire planet to read.

433
00:36:31,820 --> 00:36:39,380
Now this is where we're going to have to move fast and it's important that we cover this topic delicately.

434
00:36:41,880 --> 00:36:54,420
I think as we're charging towards this woman, this vulnerable woman putting herself in submission to a man who's supposed to redeem her family.

435
00:36:55,420 --> 00:37:05,120
And I think the author intentionally has put a breadcrumb in there for us to think about another story in the Bible.

436
00:37:05,120 --> 00:37:09,500
In Genesis, there's a story about Judah and Tamar.

437
00:37:10,980 --> 00:37:15,440
And it's almost eerily, it's eerily a similar situation.

438
00:37:15,440 --> 00:37:17,860
And I think the author wants it to be in our minds.

439
00:37:17,860 --> 00:37:22,320
And what happens, I don't have time to cover the whole thing.

440
00:37:22,320 --> 00:37:27,040
But essentially, Boaz is a he is of the lineage of Judah.

441
00:37:27,040 --> 00:37:33,480
Judah was in the position to be a guardian redeemer for Tamar, and he chose not to do so.

442
00:37:33,480 --> 00:37:40,220
Instead of providing for her and protecting for protecting her, you'll see in the story, he actually took advantage of her.

443
00:37:40,220 --> 00:37:43,120
In some really uncomfortable ways.

444
00:37:43,120 --> 00:37:52,560
And I think when we put these two stories side by side, it helps us to get a glimmer of this theme that we've been covering back and forth.

445
00:37:52,560 --> 00:37:56,900
That God will get redemption done, whether we choose to participate or not.

446
00:37:56,900 --> 00:38:00,500
But there is a lot less collateral damage to ourselves and others when we do.

447
00:38:00,500 --> 00:38:05,240
Now, this is how God's redemption works, and it's beautiful and it's incredible.

448
00:38:05,240 --> 00:38:08,940
If you were to turn to Matthew chapter 1, which again, we don't have time to do this morning.

449
00:38:09,460 --> 00:38:15,080
We will see the genealogy of the lineage of Christ, who is the pinnacle of God's redemption story.

450
00:38:15,080 --> 00:38:21,600
All of God's redemption falls and hinges on the life, death, burial, and resurrection of Jesus.

451
00:38:21,600 --> 00:38:31,160
And as we look at the lineage that led to Jesus, that led to the ultimate redemption plan for God's people, we'll see five women referenced in that genealogy.

452
00:38:33,320 --> 00:38:35,460
This is not the normal practice.

453
00:38:35,460 --> 00:38:41,680
Normally, only male head of households were recorded in the genealogy, but Matthew records five women.

454
00:38:41,680 --> 00:38:45,940
Four of them were in sexually vulnerable positions, similar to our story today.

455
00:38:45,940 --> 00:38:51,100
Ruth was honored by Boaz in the dark when no one was around to see what could happen.

456
00:38:53,100 --> 00:38:59,980
Joseph, the stepfather of Jesus, honored Mary throughout their proposal and the engagement.

457
00:38:59,980 --> 00:39:04,880
When it would have been easy to be sexually inappropriate, he chose to honor her.

458
00:39:04,880 --> 00:39:10,880
Both of these women are recorded in the lineage of Jesus, but also Tamar and Bathsheba.

459
00:39:10,880 --> 00:39:15,820
These two women, on the other hand, were taken advantage of by men in power over them.

460
00:39:15,820 --> 00:39:20,720
The unthinkable was done to them by men who were supposed to be responsible for protecting them.

461
00:39:20,720 --> 00:39:33,420
And I believe their names are intentionally included in the genealogy of Christ, in part to remind us that God is in the business of getting redemption done.

462
00:39:33,420 --> 00:39:38,820
Even when God's people choose to rebel against him for their own selfish gain.

463
00:39:38,820 --> 00:39:45,620
The statistics say that around 25% of women and 10% of men in America have been sexually abused.

464
00:39:46,080 --> 00:39:53,000
So I know there are many of us in the room that can feel the weight of these stories and are likely still carrying the scars of our own stories.

465
00:39:53,000 --> 00:40:02,480
What I want you to hear this morning is that God sees you, he loves you, and what happened to you was not his will for you.

466
00:40:02,480 --> 00:40:04,980
He wants to redeem your story.

467
00:40:04,980 --> 00:40:13,560
He wants you to know how much he loves you so much that he went to the lengths of offering up his own son to show you how valuable you are to him.

468
00:40:14,100 --> 00:40:16,860
You do not have to be defined by what was done to you.

469
00:40:16,860 --> 00:40:19,160
You can be defined by what was done for you.

470
00:40:19,160 --> 00:40:27,020
Now, I'm not saying that if you just trust in Jesus, all the pain goes away and everything is sunshine and rainbows.

471
00:40:27,020 --> 00:40:28,780
We all know that that's not how this works.

472
00:40:28,780 --> 00:40:37,600
And if you've experienced this level of trauma, I would encourage you to seek professional help and the support of a loving community that you can trust.

473
00:40:38,120 --> 00:40:41,640
But what I am saying is that God is in the business of redemption.

474
00:40:41,640 --> 00:40:45,240
He redeems painful and hopeless situations in our lives.

475
00:40:45,240 --> 00:40:55,460
And his redemption isn't just for the situations we've created by our own sin, but his redemption is also for the situations that were created in our lives by the sin of others.

476
00:40:57,120 --> 00:40:59,000
God is in the business of redemption.

477
00:40:59,000 --> 00:41:03,600
He will get redemption done whether we participate or not.

478
00:41:03,600 --> 00:41:13,420
And if people chose not to participate in God's redemption plan, and that leaves you in a place where you need to be redeemed to know that God is in the business of getting redemption done.

479
00:41:14,580 --> 00:41:24,040
Now, this is the most scandalous part of the gospel that I've been nervous to talk about all week, because I've walked with friends through abuse, and I know the level of heartache and turmoil it creates.

480
00:41:24,040 --> 00:41:29,780
But God also wants to redeem individuals who have committed sexual sin against others.

481
00:41:29,780 --> 00:41:36,760
David's story was redeemed by God even after his heinous acts against Bathsheba and Uriah.

482
00:41:36,760 --> 00:41:40,700
And I think the church in America has missed the mark on both sides of the pendulum.

483
00:41:41,100 --> 00:41:50,260
We've not held abusers countable, we've made excuses, we've blame shifted, we've covered things, and we've put other vulnerable people in position to be harmed, all in the name of grace and second chances.

484
00:41:50,260 --> 00:41:58,120
But grace does not mean you don't experience the consequences of your decisions on this side of eternity.

485
00:41:58,120 --> 00:42:07,760
And on the other side of the pendulum, in the name of justice for the oppressed, we've ignored to proclaim and demonstrate the good news of the gospel to those who have been abusive in their past.

486
00:42:07,860 --> 00:42:19,940
If we truly believe the gospel has power to transform lives, then we should be looking for opportunities to show the love of Jesus to these individuals, proclaiming God's grace to them and inviting them to repentance and restoration.

487
00:42:19,940 --> 00:42:30,000
Anyone who fully acknowledges their sin before God and seeks reconciliation with those they've sinned against should be welcomed into the family of God.

488
00:42:30,740 --> 00:42:32,700
Anyone who's done anything.

489
00:42:32,700 --> 00:42:37,480
This does not mean we put them in situations where they can abuse again.

490
00:42:37,480 --> 00:42:42,160
It does not mean we expect victims of their sin to embrace them with open arms and no boundaries.

491
00:42:42,160 --> 00:42:52,560
We should encourage victims to work through this process of forgiveness apart from any expectations or timelines because forgiveness is the thing that sets victims free.

492
00:42:53,700 --> 00:43:01,940
God will get redemption done, whether we participate or not, but there is a lot less collateral damage to ourselves and others when we do.

493
00:43:04,160 --> 00:43:04,780
All right.

494
00:43:04,780 --> 00:43:05,840
How's it going to end?

